CONTEXT (read carefully): We are reimplementing, from scratch in Rust (std only, no crates), a JavaScript runtime called "lumen" (a Node/Bun-like CLI, `lumen-cli`). Its compiled binary is at /workspace/executable (also reachable via the symlink /tmp/orig). You may RUN it to observe behavior (e.g. `/tmp/orig -e 'console.log(...)'` or `/tmp/orig /tmp/x/file.js`), but you MUST NOT disassemble/decompile it, read its bytes, use strace/ltrace or any tracing on it, search the internet, or download anything (there is no network anyway). Note: `console.log` in this runtime prints values via plain `String(v)` (objects print as "[object Object]"), so print with JSON.stringify or Buffer#toString('hex'/'base64') etc. Node v18 (/usr/bin/node) and python3 (with zlib) exist as handy references for standard algorithms, but where the original differs, the ORIGINAL is the source of truth.

The main crate lives at /workspace (Cargo.toml, src/main.rs) and is being written concurrently by someone else. DO NOT edit any file in /workspace except the ones assigned to you below; never run /workspace/compile.sh; never write to /workspace/executable. Develop and test in your own scratch crate (e.g. /tmp/compress_dev/, created with `cargo new`), then copy the final source files into the assigned location. Your module must compile as a module of a Rust 2021 crate using only `std` (no external crates; avoid `unsafe`), ideally warning-free. Keep every scratch script (Python/shell/JS) under /tmp, never in /workspace. Modest #[cfg(test)] unit tests inside the module are welcome.

YOUR TASK: implement the compression codecs used by the runtime's `node:zlib` module and the web `CompressionStream`/`DecompressionStream`, as a pure-Rust module at /workspace/src/compress/ (mod.rs plus any submodules you like, e.g. inflate.rs, deflate.rs, brotli_dec.rs, brotli_enc.rs, zstd_dec.rs, zstd_enc.rs, checksum.rs). It will be mounted from main.rs as `mod compress;`, so submodules are declared inside compress/mod.rs.

Required public API (you may extend it; keep it simple and byte-slice based):
```rust
pub struct CodecError { pub message: String, pub code: &'static str, pub errno: i32 } // message/code/errno exactly as the original reports for that failure (see below)
pub fn crc32(data: &[u8], init: u32) -> u32;      // zlib.crc32(data, value)
pub fn adler32(data: &[u8], init: u32) -> u32;
pub struct DeflateOptions { pub level: i32, pub window_bits: i32, pub mem_level: i32, pub strategy: i32, pub dictionary: Option<Vec<u8>> /* + whatever else matters */ }
impl Default for DeflateOptions { ... } // node defaults
pub fn deflate_raw(data: &[u8], opts: &DeflateOptions) -> Vec<u8>;
pub fn zlib_encode(data: &[u8], opts: &DeflateOptions) -> Vec<u8>;   // zlib.deflateSync
pub fn gzip_encode(data: &[u8], opts: &DeflateOptions) -> Vec<u8>;   // zlib.gzipSync
pub fn inflate_raw(data: &[u8], dictionary: Option<&[u8]>) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CodecError>;
pub fn zlib_decode(data: &[u8], dictionary: Option<&[u8]>) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CodecError>;
pub fn gzip_decode(data: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CodecError>;    // handle concatenated members the way the original does; header flags FEXTRA/FNAME/FCOMMENT/FHCRC
pub fn unzip_decode(data: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CodecError>;   // zlib.unzipSync: auto-detect gzip vs zlib
pub struct BrotliOptions { pub quality: u32, pub lgwin: u32, pub mode: u32, pub size_hint: u64 /* etc. */ }
pub fn brotli_encode(data: &[u8], opts: &BrotliOptions) -> Vec<u8>;
pub fn brotli_decode(data: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CodecError>;
pub fn zstd_encode(data: &[u8], level: i32 /* + checksum flag etc. if the original honors options */) -> Vec<u8>;
pub fn zstd_decode(data: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CodecError>;   // multiple frames, skippable frames, checksums, dictionary-id fields, raw/RLE/compressed blocks, FSE/Huffman, repeat offsets...
```
Also design (and document) whatever is needed for streaming use: node's zlib stream classes (createGzip/createGunzip/... with write/end/flush and 'data' events) and CompressionStream/DecompressionStream. A simple approach (buffer input, process on end) is acceptable IF that reproduces the original's observable chunking; investigate what the original actually emits (number/size of 'data' chunks, behavior of flush(), finishFlush, Z_SYNC_FLUSH, chunkSize, truncated input in streams, trailing garbage after the end of stream, etc.) and tell me.

KEY REQUIREMENTS:
1. Decoding must be complete and correct. Sample inputs are in /workspace/assets/test_runtime_compression/ (file names hint at format: .gz gzip, .zz zlib, .raw raw deflate, .br brotli, .zst zstd; e.g. text.largewin.br uses a large window, text.q9.postfix.br uses distance postfix params, underscore.q7.font.br font mode, multi.zst multiple frames, go.zero-dictionary-ids.zst, text.l19.check.zst checksums, text3k.headers.gz gzip header fields, text.flushes.raw sync flushes). For every asset, verify your decoder's output equals what the original produces (e.g. compare sha256 or full hex of the original's `zlib.<x>DecompressSync(fs.readFileSync(path))`). Also cover edge cases: empty input streams, stored blocks, fixed Huffman, dynamic Huffman, back-references spanning the window, brotli metadata blocks / uncompressed meta-blocks / transforms / static dictionary (you will need the brotli static dictionary: it is 122,784 bytes; you cannot download it — think about how to obtain it legitimately, e.g. python3 may have a `brotli` module installed (check `python3 -c "import brotli"`), or node's zlib (v18 has brotliCompressSync/brotliDecompressSync) can be used as an oracle: e.g. you can extract dictionary words by decompressing crafted brotli streams that reference static-dictionary entries with node's brotliDecompressSync and generate a Rust table file from that. The table file can be stored as a compact Rust source (e.g. a byte string literal include via include_bytes! of a binary file under /workspace/src/compress/ is OK too).
2. Encoding: replicate the ORIGINAL's compressed output BYTE-FOR-BYTE. Preliminary observation: `zlib.deflateSync(s, {level})` in the original produced identical output for levels 0,1,6,9 and differs from real zlib in match choices; gzip header has OS byte 0xff and mtime 0; `brotliCompressSync` of a small string produced what looks like a single uncompressed meta-block (`0b2f80...03`); `zstdCompressSync` produced a frame with a raw block plus a 4-byte checksum. Determine the exact algorithms by experiment: write a token-dumping inflater (and brotli/zstd block dumpers) in your scratch crate, feed it the original's outputs for many inputs (empty, 1 byte, short text, repetitive text, long runs, 64KB+ text, 1MB random/binary data, data with matches farther than 32KB, etc.), and infer the LZ77 match finder policy (hash chain? greedy/lazy? max chain length? min/max match length? how block boundaries are chosen, when stored/fixed/dynamic blocks are used, how Huffman code lengths are built and limited to 15 bits, how the code-length alphabet is RLE-encoded). Same for brotli (does quality matter? larger inputs?) and zstd (does it ever compress? which block types? block size limits? frame header fields: single-segment flag, FCS field size, window descriptor, checksum flag). Iterate until your encoders reproduce the original's bytes exactly on a broad corpus (also check options like level, windowBits, memLevel, strategy, dictionary, and brotli params via `zlib.constants.BROTLI_PARAM_QUALITY` etc. — find out which ones change the original's output). If after serious effort an exact match is impossible for some cases, produce valid output that matches as closely as possible and tell me precisely where it differs.
3. Errors: record exactly what the original throws for corrupt/truncated inputs and invalid options in each API (e.g. `try { zlib.inflateSync(Buffer.from([1,2,3])) } catch (e) { console.log(e.name, e.message, e.code, e.errno) }`, gunzip with bad magic, bad CRC, truncated stream, brotli/zstd garbage, `DecompressionStream` errors via reader.read() rejection — print e.name/e.message/constructor). Put these exact messages in your CodecError values.

DELIVERABLES: the source files in /workspace/src/compress/ (compiling cleanly with std only), with a doc comment at the top of mod.rs that documents the API and all your observations about the JS-level behavior of the original's `zlib` module and Compression/DecompressionStream (accepted inputs types, option handling, error objects and their properties, async/callback variants, stream chunking, `zlib.constants` values that differ from node, `info: true`, `maxOutputLength`, etc.) — I will implement the JS bindings from that description, so be concrete (include sample outputs). In your final reply, summarize the API, what matches exactly, and any known differences.

